<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">King Kuai of Yan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 314 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">King Kuai of Yan was ruler of the State of Yan in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>. Persuaded to imitate the legendary sage-kings, he abdicated in favour of his minister Zizhi in about 316 BCE. The move provoked civil war in Yan and an invasion by Qi in 314 BCE, in which both Kuai and Zizhi were killed, and the episode became a cautionary tale in Chinese political thought.</p></div>
